from author import *
from rag_api import lab
lab(22,'identity','Conversation ownership, expiry and bounded storage',
'''A session identifier is a reference, not proof of ownership. Scope conversation state by authenticated tenant, principal and session, then revalidate access on every turn. Otherwise a guessed session ID can expose another user's history. Expiry and deletion are data-governance contracts as well as memory controls.\n\nThe fixture service uses explicit synthetic principals and a bounded in-memory store. This demonstrates scope and lifecycle mechanics without claiming a production identity provider or durable database.''',
'''The Conversations class uses a tuple key, expiry timestamps, a maximum session count and a lock around updates. It retains at most twelve messages. New sessions at capacity are rejected visibly; expired sessions are removed before admission.\n\nNever accept tenant/user from model arguments to choose state. For a persistent deployment, store the same ownership fields and apply retention/deletion to messages, summaries and cached evidence. Recheck document permissions before using prior evidence.''',
'''Two tenants reuse session s and the implementation keys only by s. A summary survives after the messages were deleted. Concurrent turns lose one user's update because state mutation is not synchronized.\n\nTest cross-tenant and cross-principal access, expiry boundaries, deletion, capacity and concurrent updates. A session may be valid while its prior evidence is no longer authorized.''',
'''Acceptance shows isolated histories, bounded growth and explicit lifecycle outcomes. The local tests use an injected clock so expiry is deterministic and fast.\n\nSDETs can apply stateful API test discipline: establish identity and preconditions, issue a turn, inspect the allowed state change and verify an unauthorized principal cannot observe or modify it.''',
"key=(tenant,user,session)\n# Existing messages are not retrieved with session ID alone\n# local-labs/quality/chat.py bounds state and enforces expiry",
"def session_access(record,tenant,user,now):\n    return record['tenant']==tenant and record['user']==user and record['expires']>now",
"r={'tenant':'a','user':'u','expires':10}\nassert session_access(r,'a','u',9)\nassert not session_access(r,'b','u',9)\nassert not session_access(r,'a','u',10)","def session_access(record,tenant,user,now):\n    return False",
('What does a session ID alone establish?',['Only a reference, not ownership','Authenticated identity','Permanent permission to old evidence'],0,'Ownership comes from authenticated scope and access validation.'),['Session ownership','Retention'])
lab(22,'followups','Multi-turn intent: resolve references or ask a clarification',
'''A follow-up can depend on a prior subject: explain that, what about the next build, or does it affect checkout. Resolving the reference is part of the task contract. When multiple subjects are possible, clarification is safer and more useful than silently choosing one.\n\nEarlier assistant statements are not authoritative facts. Retain the last validated user subject and evidence references separately from generated prose, especially when compressing history.''',
'''The deterministic fixture chatbot detects a bounded set of pronoun follow-ups, reuses the last supported user topic and performs retrieval again under current tenant permissions. With no known subject, it returns a clarification status and no citations. This policy is labeled as a fixture baseline, not general language understanding.\n\nBuild multi-turn cases with topic switches, missing subjects and contradictory evidence. A live model can classify intent or propose a rewrite, but the application validates subject IDs, permissions and operation scope before execution.''',
'''A summarizer changes may be a database issue into confirmed database issue. A follow-up about that uses another session's subject. A topic switch leaves the old subject active and produces an irrelevant answer.\n\nTest the resolved query/subject as well as final answer quality. Keep uncertainty in summaries, and ensure no-answer turns do not fabricate a new subject that later turns treat as verified.''',
'''Acceptance includes expected clarification, current-scope retrieval and explicit subject provenance for each turn. Score context retention, contradiction and supported task completion separately.\n\nFor SDETs, a conversation is a sequence of state transitions with assertions at every step. A final plausible answer does not prove earlier ambiguity or ownership was handled correctly.''',
"# First turn: 'Explain that' -> clarification\n# After supported 'retry HTTP 429': 'Explain that' -> scoped re-retrieval\npython -m pytest local-labs/tests/test_quality.py -k conversation",
"def resolve(subject,followup,is_reference):\n    if is_reference and not subject:return {'status':'clarification','query':None}\n    return {'status':'ready','query':subject+' '+followup if is_reference else followup}",
"assert resolve(None,'that',True)['status']=='clarification'\nassert resolve('HTTP 429','explain that',True)['query']=='HTTP 429 explain that'\nassert resolve('old','new topic',False)['query']=='new topic'","def resolve(subject,followup,is_reference):\n    return {}",
('Which prior content should ground a follow-up?',['Validated subject/evidence, not unsupported assistant claims','Any earlier confident sentence','Another user’s session summary'],0,'The state must preserve provenance and uncertainty.'),['Reference resolution','Clarification'])
lab(22,'packing','Bound message history without losing the evidence contract',
'''Message history grows with every turn and competes with current instructions, retrieved evidence and output budget. A bounded context policy chooses what to retain and what to summarize. Dropping messages can remove a necessary constraint; summaries can distort uncertainty or citations.\n\nUse the model's tokenizer for production token budgets. Character count and message count are useful teaching controls but must be labeled as approximations.''',
'''Reserve tokens for instructions and completion, include the latest task and authorized evidence, then select relevant prior turns. Store subject/evidence state outside the compressed prose so permission and uncertainty checks remain explicit.\n\nThe fixture store caps message count and re-retrieves evidence; it does not claim token-optimal context selection. For a live adapter, count the actual serialized request and reject or clarify when essential evidence cannot fit instead of truncating blindly.''',
'''A last-N-message policy drops the original user restriction but retains a long assistant explanation. A summary includes a revoked source. Context packing silently excludes the only relevant chunk while the report blames generation.\n\nTest long conversations, essential early constraints, missing sources and permission changes. Retain packed evidence IDs and context policy version in traces.''',
'''Acceptance proves bounded history and preservation of essential task/evidence fields. Compare task completion before and after compression on paired multi-turn cases.\n\nSDETs should separate storage retention from model context selection: a message may remain stored but not be supplied to a model. Both policies need explicit tests and deletion behavior.''',
"messages=messages[-12:]\n# A production token budget must count serialized messages with the chosen tokenizer",
"def retain_messages(messages,limit):\n    if limit<0:raise ValueError('limit')\n    return messages[-limit:] if limit else []",
"assert retain_messages([1,2,3],2)==[2,3]\nassert retain_messages([1],0)==[]\ntry:retain_messages([], -1);assert False\nexcept ValueError:pass","def retain_messages(messages,limit):\n    return messages",
('Is a message-count cap a production token budget?',['No: tokenization and serialized content must be counted','Yes: every message has equal length','Only for assistant messages'],0,'Message sizes vary; actual model input requires tokenizer-aware accounting.'),['Conversation summary','Context budget'])
lab(22,'response-state','Response states: answer, clarification, abstention and failure',
'''A chatbot has more valid outcomes than a fluent answer. Clarification requests missing intent information; abstention indicates insufficient authorized evidence; refusal handles a disallowed request; unavailable/degraded status describes a dependency problem. Mixing these states makes user behavior and evaluation ambiguous.\n\nStreaming presentation is provisional until a complete response passes validation. Partial text must not be cached or stored as a verified final answer.''',
'''Use a typed response with status, answer, citations, mode and session reference. Assert status-specific invariants: an answered result has supporting citations, an abstained result has no invented answer, and a clarification asks for the missing subject.\n\nA streaming client tracks delta events, completion and error, then commits only the validated final object. Persist request IDs to prevent duplicate turn insertion on retry. Cancellation must release provider and session resources.''',
'''A network failure is presented as no evidence, hiding the real outage. An incomplete stream is marked answered because its first token arrived. A retried request appends the same question twice.\n\nTest state mapping, truncated streams, duplicate request IDs and cancellation. Ensure the UI gives a visible path to clarify or retry without implying a completed task.''',
'''Acceptance tests assert state and provenance in addition to answer semantics. Report counts by outcome so a high abstention rate cannot hide poor task completion, and an outage cannot look like safe uncertainty.\n\nFor SDETs, stable outer response states are deterministic API contracts even when the model-generated inner text varies. These states support useful review and production alerts.''',
"response={'status':'abstained','answer':None,'citations':[]}\nassert response['answer'] is None",
"def response_valid(r):\n    status=r.get('status')\n    if status=='answered':return bool(r.get('answer')) and bool(r.get('citations'))\n    if status=='abstained':return r.get('answer') is None and not r.get('citations')\n    if status=='clarification':return bool(r.get('answer')) and not r.get('citations')\n    return False",
"assert response_valid({'status':'answered','answer':'x','citations':['D1']})\nassert not response_valid({'status':'answered','answer':'x','citations':[]})\nassert response_valid({'status':'abstained','answer':None,'citations':[]})","def response_valid(r):\n    return False",
('When can a streaming answer become the durable result?',['After completion and validation','At the first delta','When it sounds confident'],0,'Partial streams can fail or contain invalid/incomplete data.'),['Response state','Partial result'])
lab(30,'models','Request and response models: strict types at the boundary',
'''A request model defines which fields a client may supply, their types and bounds. A response model defines which domain fields may leave the service. Forbid extras when unknown fields could smuggle credentials, tenant identity or operational controls. Type validation and business invariants remain separate layers.\n\nStrictness must be deliberate. Coercing a numeric string for ingestion can be useful, but coercing an approval flag from a nonempty string can authorize the wrong action.''',
'''Use Pydantic ConfigDict(extra='forbid'), bounded strings and closed mode/category enums. Validate live provider outputs again at the domain boundary, resolve evidence IDs and apply UNKNOWN/review rules. Response filtering prevents accidental internal fields from leaking.\n\nThe fixture API rejects an api_key field in classification requests and derives tenant identity through a dependency. Source IDs select the immutable authorized log rather than trusting a caller's unrelated text.''',
'''A service accepts any dictionary and serializes the provider request with credentials. A permissive model ignores unsupported fields, leaving clients unaware that their intended control was not applied. A valid category lacks evidence.\n\nTest extra fields, wrong types, oversized strings, invalid modes and inconsistent source IDs. Keep error messages stable and free of secret values.''',
'''Acceptance includes request rejection, response schema conformance and domain evidence checks. OpenAPI documents the same contract that integration tests observe.\n\nSDETs should distinguish 422 input failures from 403 authorization and 503 dependency failures. A typed AI service keeps its outer contract predictable even when an inner model is uncertain.''',
"from pydantic import BaseModel,ConfigDict,Field\nclass Input(BaseModel):\n    model_config=ConfigDict(extra='forbid')\n    log:str=Field(min_length=1,max_length=10000)",
"def request_fields(body):\n    return set(body)<={'log','failure_id','mode'} and isinstance(body.get('log'),str) and 1<=len(body['log'])<=10000",
"assert request_fields({'log':'x'})\nassert not request_fields({'log':'x','api_key':'secret'})\nassert not request_fields({'log':''})","def request_fields(body):\n    return False",
('What should supply the request’s tenant identity?',['Authenticated dependency context','A model-generated field','Any caller-provided dictionary key'],0,'The service owns authenticated scope; inputs cannot grant access.'),['Response model','Strict validation'])
lab(30,'dependencies','Dependency injection and realistic API test overrides',
'''Dependency injection separates routes from concrete provider, retrieval and identity implementations. A test can substitute a controlled adapter while retaining real request parsing, routing and response serialization. This is stronger than testing the route function in isolation with hand-built objects.\n\nOverrides should replace external boundaries, not bypass the application invariant being tested. Mocking authorization to always true cannot establish tenant isolation.''',
'''Use FastAPI Depends for principal and adapter construction. In tests, supply a fixture provider returning known valid, malformed or refused results, then issue HTTP requests through TestClient/ASGI transport. Assert status, body schema, headers and actual domain effects.\n\nReset overrides after each test and create fresh state when histories or caches are involved. Keep a separate integration test for actual SDK/protocol wiring where credentials are not required.''',
'''A global dependency override leaks into later tests and disables auth. Tests call classify directly and miss request model validation. A mock returns a convenient dictionary incompatible with the real provider’s typed result.\n\nTest the normalized adapter contract and at least one real serialization path. Include invalid inputs and dependency exceptions so public error behavior is checked.''',
'''Acceptance proves that the fixture and live adapters share the same domain interface, while their execution modes remain visible. The live adapter test validates the request configuration and closes the async client without making a paid call.\n\nSDETs should retain realistic boundaries: mocking a provider is appropriate for deterministic service behavior; evaluating model correctness requires controlled live evidence.''',
"from fastapi.testclient import TestClient\nclient=TestClient(app)\nresponse=client.post('/chat',json={'query':'retry HTTP 429'})\nassert response.status_code==200 and response.json()['citations']",
"def normalize_adapter(result):\n    required={'category','evidence_ids','needs_review'}\n    if not required<=set(result):raise ValueError('adapter contract')\n    return {k:result[k] for k in sorted(required)}",
"assert normalize_adapter({'category':'UNKNOWN','evidence_ids':[],'needs_review':True})['needs_review']\ntry:normalize_adapter({'category':'UNKNOWN'});assert False\nexcept ValueError:pass","def normalize_adapter(result):\n    return {}",
('Which boundary should a deterministic service test replace?',['The external provider while retaining validation/policy','Every application function','Authorization with an unconditional pass'],0,'Controlled external results let real application contracts be exercised.'),['Dependency override','ASGI test'])
lab(30,'async-work','Async service capacity: provider I/O versus CPU work',
'''Async awaits allow other requests to progress while I/O is pending. They do not make blocking CPU or synchronous network operations nonblocking. Model training and long evaluation should use bounded jobs/workers, while request-time provider calls use asynchronous clients, deadlines and admission controls.\n\nPer-attempt timeouts are not a whole-task deadline. Queue waiting and retries must fit the user's total latency budget.''',
'''The live adapter uses AsyncOpenAI, bounded SDK retries, a maximum output budget and asyncio.wait_for over the entire call. It closes the client in finally. A production service also bounds queued/in-flight work and cancels provider work on client disconnect.\n\nKeep lightweight fixture classification in the route and move expensive fitting into the explicit training commands. Record queue, provider and validation durations separately rather than one unexplained timer.''',
'''An async handler invokes a blocking trainer and starves unrelated health requests. A timeout is caught as success with an empty result. Cancellation does not release a semaphore permit.\n\nInject a slow adapter and verify deadline outcome, cleanup and subsequent healthy requests. Load tests should include concurrent long/short requests and errors, not only the fastest endpoint.''',
'''Acceptance demonstrates bounded external waits, clean cancellation and stable typed unavailable responses. CPU-heavy work has a separate controlled execution path.\n\nSDETs should assess both latency and correctness under concurrency. A service returning invalid results quickly does not meet performance objectives, and a correct result after the deadline does not complete the intended user task.''',
"import asyncio\nresult=await asyncio.wait_for(provider_call(),timeout=15)\n# Close clients and release capacity in finally blocks",
"def can_admit(in_flight,max_in_flight,queue,max_queue):\n    return in_flight<max_in_flight or queue<max_queue",
"assert can_admit(1,2,0,0)\nassert not can_admit(2,2,3,3)\nassert can_admit(2,2,2,3)","def can_admit(in_flight,max_in_flight,queue,max_queue):\n    return False",
('Does declaring async make CPU training nonblocking?',['No: it needs a separate bounded execution path','Yes: all work becomes concurrent','Only if training loss decreases'],0,'Async schedules waiting; CPU work still consumes execution resources.'),['Admission control','Async I/O'])
lab(30,'operations','Health, readiness, OpenAPI and safe error contracts',
'''Liveness asks whether the process is functioning; readiness asks whether it can serve its required workload. A live process can be unready because a dependency or index is unavailable. OpenAPI describes route contracts for clients, while actual integration checks prove that implementation matches them.\n\nHealth probes should be inexpensive and avoid paid model calls. Quality evaluation is a different workflow from operational probing.''',
'''Expose a cheap health result and a readiness result naming required mode/dependency state. Test all domain endpoints and request/response models against OpenAPI. Add correlation and timing headers without exposing credentials or raw sensitive errors.\n\nMap malformed input to 422, denied identity/access to 401/403 and controlled dependency failure to 503. Retain internal safe trace references for diagnosis, but do not serialize raw provider exceptions to clients.''',
'''A readiness probe always returns ready despite an unavailable required index. A health probe calls the model, causing costs and false restarts during rate limits. Error bodies leak request headers or tokens.\n\nTest health under simulated dependency failure, readiness transitions, public error schemas and secret redaction. Check that every advertised route can be exercised with a known fixture.''',
'''Acceptance includes cheap probes, accurate readiness for the declared mode, typed OpenAPI and safe correlated errors. Fixture readiness states must not be mistaken for live-provider readiness.\n\nFor SDETs, probe and API checks are deterministic operational contracts. They support deployment and recovery while quality metrics separately assess the model's task behavior.''',
"# GET /health -> process status\n# GET /ready -> readiness for declared fixture/live dependencies\n# Inspect /openapi.json and correlate X-Request-ID with safe traces",
"def readiness(required):\n    return bool(required) and all(value is True for value in required.values())",
"assert readiness({'data':True,'adapter':True})\nassert not readiness({'data':True,'adapter':False})\nassert not readiness({})","def readiness(required):\n    return False",
('Should liveness issue a paid model generation?',['No: keep it cheap and operational','Yes: every probe measures quality','Only with high temperature'],0,'Operational probes and model-quality evaluations have different purposes.'),['Liveness','OpenAPI','Safe error'])
